{%- comment -%}
  How many entries are left, taken from live Shopify inventory.

  THE INVENTORY IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH, NOT custom.entries_sold.

  `entries_sold` is a hand-maintained metafield. Nothing writes to it, so it
  still reads 0 while real orders exist against a competition. A figure that
  only moves when someone remembers to move it is a figure that will be wrong
  in public, on the most repeated component on the site. Inventory, by
  contrast, is decremented transactionally by Shopify on every order, and the
  cap is enforced against it at checkout. So:

      taken     = entries_total - inventory_quantity
      remaining = inventory_quantity

  POSTAL ENTRIES AND THE OVERSELL CASE.

  Not every order path respects the cap. A customer checkout does: Shopify
  refuses a checkout whose stock has gone, with MERCHANDISE_OUT_OF_STOCK.
  A Draft Order does not — completing two against a stock of 1 succeeded and
  left inventory at -1, verified against the live store. Postal entries are
  processed as Draft Orders, so a postal entry taken after the cap is reached
  will drive inventory negative.

  That makes a negative `inventory_quantity` a real state, not a theoretical
  one, and it must never reach a customer as "-1 entries left". Both figures
  are therefore clamped: remaining never drops below zero, and taken never
  exceeds the published cap. A competition at or past its cap reports itself
  as fully entered instead of quoting a number.

  The reverse case is clamped too. Raising a cap, or restocking, can leave
  inventory above entries_total, which would otherwise render a negative
  number taken.

  Nothing renders unless the cap is a real number above zero AND Shopify is
  tracking inventory for the variant. No cap, no tracking, no output — there
  is no fallback and no estimate.

  Parameters:
    product  (required)
    mode     'left'  "2,570 of 4,000 entries left"   (default, used on cards)
             'cap'   "4,000 entries"                  (the published total only)
    class    extra class names for the wrapper
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign total = product.metafields.custom.entries_total.value
  assign variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
  assign tracked = false
  if variant.inventory_management == 'shopify'
    assign tracked = true
  endif

  assign show = false
  if total != blank and total > 0
    assign show = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if show -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign display_mode = mode | default: 'left'
    capture total_text
      render 'format-number', value: total
    endcapture

    comment
      Without tracking there is no live figure to quote, so only the published
      cap can be stated. It is still true, it just says nothing about what is
      left.
    endcomment
    if tracked == false
      assign display_mode = 'cap'
    endif

    if display_mode != 'cap'
      assign stock = variant.inventory_quantity

      comment
        Clamp both ends. Below zero is the Draft Order oversell case described
        above; above the cap is a restock or a raised cap.
      endcomment
      assign remaining = stock
      if remaining < 0
        assign remaining = 0
      endif
      if remaining > total
        assign remaining = total
      endif

      capture remaining_text
        render 'format-number', value: remaining
      endcapture
    endif
  -%}

  <p class="competition-availability num {{ class }}">
    {%- if display_mode == 'cap' -%}
      {{ 'products.competition.entry_cap' | t: total: total_text }}
    {%- elsif remaining == 0 -%}
      {{ 'products.competition.fully_entered' | t }}
    {%- else -%}
      {{ 'products.competition.entries_left' | t: remaining: remaining_text, total: total_text }}
    {%- endif -%}
  </p>
{%- endif -%}
